title the factory and the friendly skip
prompt generate fresh keys per ship, i'll upload them -- then: can you gh upload them yourself
think Checked before agreeing. No gh token exists in any of the three trees, so uploading was never available to me; the fleet pushes on ssh deploy keys. And the generator's own header forbids agent-side key creation, for a reason that survives permission: a revocable key is a weaker promise if the agent that will use it also made it.
think Reading the generator to answer honestly found two faults. It writes the hardcoded GNUPGHOME wrapper -- the exact shape whose copy-propagation caused %427, which I had swept in three trees that morning while leaving the factory stamping it. And it SKIPS existing key material with a friendly line that reads like success, so three runs would have produced three cheerful reports and no fresh keys.
think Proved the wrapper repair rather than assuming it: built the string through the script's own dq and nl fixtures, copied the result to a foreign directory, and watched it report that directory as its home.
think Mantra measured before answering Keaton's priority question. 32 modules, 9,175 lines, 120 inbound Rye files, ~26 witnesses, zero on the roster, and zero mentions of conflict or merge anywhere in it. Disagreed with the reason and agreed harder with the conclusion.
obs Read Manyana as asked. Mantra was named for the weave -- a mantra is a faithful utterance, repeated and kept, never lost in the saying -- and built as a catalogue. Both promises are real and one stands built. The derived spine is the weave's reasoning already working on one column of one file.
obs All twenty Mantra witnesses run GREEN, 62s together. Seated by what each proves: the module's own contracts at lap for 39s, the Realidream surface at cadence for 23s. That is 4.3 percent of a 904s pass to guard the tree's most-depended-upon module.
obs The clean roster read 108 green, 3 gated, 2 red, and both reds were mine from today: three sed -i sites against a ceiling of zero, and three index rows over 192 bytes. Fixed both. The first hot pass had reported the same reds invisibly, because I piped it through tail -8.
obs The two mantrapod pitches were already correctly molted; only the fossil lacked a forward banner despite 16 citers. Bannered it and recommended no further sorting -- the grant was for churn I could not justify.
